# Hand the arm to a policy for one episode.
#
# The goal is held for the whole episode: while it is active the policy node
# owns the 30 Hz loop, the safety limits and the robot command path, and it
# drives whatever prompt the manager is publishing on TaskState. Cancelling
# the goal stops the arm.
#
# An action rather than a start/stop service pair because the thing being
# asked for is long-running and has an outcome. The caller gets the
# cancellation path and the "who owns the arm right now" answer for free;
# with two Triggers both have to be rebuilt, and a caller that dies between
# them leaves the arm running.
#
# Only one goal is accepted at a time. A second is rejected rather than
# queued -- there is one arm, and a queued episode would start whenever the
# first happened to end.

# Checkpoint to run. Empty uses the one the node was configured with. A
# different path triggers a load, which takes seconds and is reported through
# feedback before the episode starts.
string checkpoint

# Overrides the configured speed_scale when > 0. Every velocity is multiplied
# by it. First runs on hardware belong at 0.3 or below.
float32 speed_scale

# Episode wall-clock limit, seconds. <= 0 uses the configured
# max_episode_s. On expiry the policy aborts itself and zeroes the arm.
float32 max_episode_s

# For SmolVLA, the task sentence to run, exactly as trained. Ignored for
# MVLA, whose prompt comes from the manager through TaskState -- an MVLA
# prompt is assembled per subtask and cannot be fixed at goal time.
string task_instruction
---
# False when the episode ended any way other than the manager or the caller
# stopping it cleanly: a load failure, a lost observation stream, the
# episode timeout, or the TaskState deadman.
bool success

# Terminal state and reason, mirroring the last PolicyState.
uint8 state
string reason

# Control ticks executed, and how many of them sent a nonzero velocity.
# A large gap between the two means the episode spent its time held at zero
# by a safety limit or a stale observation.
uint32 ticks
uint32 moving_ticks

# Where the per-tick log was written (one JSON object per line: command,
# action, flags, head values, events). Empty if logging was off.
string log_path
---
# Loading stages before the episode starts: "loading" | "ready", then
# "running". Rate-limited to ~5 Hz once running; PolicyState carries the
# full per-tick detail.
string stage

uint8 state
float32 elapsed_s
int32 chunk_index
float32 latency_s
float32 value_episode
